{% extends "pages/_guide.jinja" %}
{% set guide_slug = 'steps' %}
{% set thesis = 'A step definition is an ordinary Rust function with a pattern on it. The pattern decides which lines it runs for and what it pulls out of them.' %}
{% set sections = [
  {'id': 'patterns', 'label': 'Patterns'},
  {'id': 'placeholders', 'label': 'Typed placeholders'},
  {'id': 'inferred', 'label': 'Inferred patterns and expr'},
  {'id': 'returns', 'label': 'Steps that return'},
  {'id': 'fine-print', 'label': 'Fine print'},
] %}
{% block guide %}
          <section id="patterns" aria-labelledby="patterns-title">
            <h2 id="patterns-title">Patterns</h2>
            <p>
              <code>#[given]</code>, <code>#[when]</code>, and
              <code>#[then]</code> each take the text of the step they
              implement, without its keyword. A pattern matches the whole
              line, not a prefix of it, and a <code>And</code> or
              <code>But</code> step is matched against the keyword it
              continues.
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/lantern.rs', sample='tests/lantern.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[given("a lantern on the trolley")]
fn load(trolley: &mut Trolley) {
    trolley.loaded = true;
    trolley.upright = true;
}
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              Parameters that are not placeholders are fixtures, found by
              name: <code>trolley</code> here is the <code>trolley</code>
              fixture. <a href="/rstest-bdd/guides/fixtures-and-state/">Fixtures and state</a>
              covers them.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="placeholders" aria-labelledby="placeholders-title">
            <h2 id="placeholders-title">Typed placeholders</h2>
            <p>
              <code>{name}</code> in a pattern captures text into the parameter
              of the same name. <code>{name:Type}</code> also constrains what
              matches and parses it through <code>FromStr</code> before the
              step runs, so the function receives a number rather than a
              string.
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/counting.rs', sample='tests/counting.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[given("a trolley carrying {count:u32} lanterns")]
fn load(trolley: &mut Trolley, count: u32) {
    trolley.lanterns = count;
}
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              Quotes in the step are part of the text, so a pattern that
              expects them says so, and the placeholder captures what is
              between them:
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/weather.rs', sample='tests/weather.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[given("the forecast is \"{forecast}\"")]
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
          </section>

          <section id="inferred" aria-labelledby="inferred-title">
            <h2 id="inferred-title">Inferred patterns and <code>expr</code></h2>
            <p>
              A step attribute with no pattern takes it from the function’s
              name, with underscores read as spaces. A pattern may also be
              given as <code>expr = "…"</code>, the form cucumber-rs uses, which
              eases moving a suite across.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/bell.feature') }}
            {{ ui.sample('tests/bell.rs') }}
          </section>

          <section id="returns" aria-labelledby="returns-title">
            <h2 id="returns-title">Steps that return</h2>
            <p>
              A step may return a value instead of mutating a fixture. The
              value replaces the one fixture of the same type for the steps
              that follow. A <code>Result</code> is unwrapped: <code>Ok</code>
              is stored, and <code>Err</code> fails the scenario at that step.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/shortbread.feature') }}
            {{ ui.sample('tests/shortbread.rs') }}
            <p>
              Here the <code>Given</code> step fills the tin, the
              <code>When</code> step hands back a new one, and the
              <code>Then</code> step reads the latest. Were four friends to
              arrive at a tin of three, the <code>When</code> step would fail
              with “only 3 biscuits for 4 friends”.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="fine-print" aria-labelledby="fine-print-title">
            <h2 id="fine-print-title">Fine print</h2>
            {% call ui.note('Matching rules', 'caveat') %}
            <p>
              A returned value replaces a fixture only when exactly one fixture
              has its type; with none, or more than one, the value is dropped
              and the fixtures stay as they were. A returned
              <code>()</code> or <code>Ok(())</code> stores nothing. To match a
              literal brace in a pattern, double it: <code>{{ '{{' }}</code> and
              <code>{{ '}}' }}</code>.
            </p>
            {% endcall %}
          </section>
{% endblock %}
